                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_426">Oxford, Oxfordshire.
                        Balliol College (Domus de Balliolo)</orgName><p>‘1 (guard-leaf) has on
                        verso, besides some early pencil notes (reversed), two erased inscriptions,
                        one of which reads: Liber domus de Baliolo in Oxon’ ex legato magistri Thome
                        (?) . . . quondam socii &lt;eiusdem domus?&gt; cuius anime propicietur deus
                        . . . alienaverit . . . et est Henricus de Gandavo. (The same false
                        attribution is given in early pencil at the head of 2: Henricus de Gandavo
                        doctor solemnis.) The volume was still in possession of the College late in
                        the 14th cent., when the inventory-mark 459 liber theologie was entered at
                        the top of 2 (there is also a valuation: precium xii s.). After that it must
                        have been alienated, and the inscription erased, for Langbaine says ‘Donum
                        Willelmi Gray’, and there is a new entry in an unknown 15th-cent. hand on
                        1v: Liber domus Ball. [...] Johannes Braxton, who scribbles his name on
                        135v, matriculated from Balliol A.D. 1581.’ (Mynors, Balliol,
                     197)</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">William Gray (c. 1414–1478), bishop of
                     Ely</provenance>
               </history>
